the resurgence of vintage video games is  great for many reasons not the least of  which is the availability of new  equipment to play old games on modern  TVs seattle-based analog has  traditionally stood ahead of the  competition by releasing great-looking  consoles that offer best-in-class video  processing and notably nuanced  configuration settings and its latest  device the super NT is no different it's  a beautifully designed compact console  that at the moment is the best device  for playing original Super Nintendo  cartridges on HD and 4k TVs it's a  relatively expensive option at $189 but  if you obsess over minor details and  strive for authenticity the super antes  price tag is easily justifiable in  addition to Nintendo's own Super  Nintendo classic there are multiple sub  $100 HD enabled Super Nintendo consoles  to choose from nowadays from  manufacturers like hyper can and retro  'but while functional the retro and  retro trios of the world are consoles  designed on a budget with constrained  feature sets an outdated tech sure you  can in theory grow accustomed to  shortcomings like input latency and  improper video processing but accepting  these quirks doesn't diminish their  existence analog took a decidedly  different approach for its console that  eliminates these issues from the start  the Super NT offers highly configurable  video output with resolutions up to  1080p and near-perfect performance  thanks to the industry first FPGA  configuration under the hood in simple  terms an FPGA chip can when programmed  correctly replicate components like RAM  video processors and CPUs a process that  causes the FPGA to literally reorient  its circuitry in this case developer  Kevin Horton has created a seemingly  complete Super Nintendo on an FPGA a  feed no other individual or company can  claim generally speaking games played on  the super NT look and play exactly as  they should  albeit up scaled to resolutions beyond  the original systems native 240p signal  the Super interval output video at 480p  576p 720p or 1080p with suitable options  to cater to the peculiarities of games  designed for both NTSC and PAL standards  however the output resolution is only  half of the story once that's settled  you can dive into other menus to  configure the aspect ratio of game  screen adjust its position crop unwanted  portions of the image and manually scale  width and height to achieve your desired  look simulated scan lines are also  available  but rather than apply a filter over the  image the super antes solution operates  on the individual horizontal lines of  resolution so when you enable scan lines  in conjunction with a resolution that is  not a multiple of 240p such as 1080p  they lack complete uniformity the super  in Tal line options can be adjusted and  slightly improved but if you want to  play with quote unquote proper scan  lines your best solution is to stick to  480p or 720p with over 2000 Super  Nintendo games released across multiple  territories it's virtually impossible  for anyone to say with absolute  certainty that the Super NT as 100%  compatible with all games from start to  finish but in testing dozens of  cartridges we only had issues with two  games  Mortal Kombat 2 and a reproduction  cartridge of the unreleased English  version of rendering Ranger are - known  as Targa analogs answer for the moral  combat - issue to fix the bug in less  than 48 hours a new firmware came  quickly after analog reproduced the  glitch on its end and it's comforting to  know that a precedent has been set for  addressing and resolving incompatibility  issues in short order Targa on the other  hand remains an issue it simply won't  get past the publisher splash page  despite working fine and other  aftermarket consoles this was also the  case when trying to play the Japanese  version using a flash card on the super  NT rendering Ranger is known to be a  problematic game even for official Super  Nintendo consoles but analog promises a  fix is on the way in the near future of  note is the potential as hinted by  analog for an option down the road to  tailor the Super n T's fpga on-the-fly  to match Super Nintendo Hardware  revisions even among Nintendo's own  consoles these variations and processors  and coders would lead to rare instances  of glitches in certain games such as  rendering Ranger r2 so it's refreshing  to know that the super NT may have a  workaround for end-user troubleshooting  at a later date aside from those two  exceptions compatibility was otherwise  rock-solid during our tests including  games like Star Fox Mega Man x2 and  Street Fighter Alpha 2 which are  designed around traditionally  troublesome special expansion chips that  handle applications like 3d calculations  and asset compression there's also  native support for peripheral carts like  game genie and super Gameboy including  streaming audio which most Super  Nintendo clones struggle with the only  accessory you can count on not working  is the super scope a light gun designed  for and only compatible with CRT TVs  analog is currently developing a quote  unquote lag-free digital to analog  attachment for the super NT to support  CRT televisions the performance release  date and cost of the DAC remains to be  seen  on the outside the super NT is  straightforward a USB micro port for  power an HDMI port for AV output an SD  card slot for firmware updates and two  Super Nintendo controller ports  traditional Super Nintendo controllers  work exactly as expected and as analog  suggests you can also use wireless  controllers made by 8-bit dough which  now come in colors designed to match the  four color variants of the Super NT the  a potato controllers are convenient to  connect and charge offers surprisingly  low latency and feel almost like a  Nintendo made controller the one minor  issue is the d-pad which will only  occasionally move your character on  screen left or right when you'd mean to  press down should you inadvertently tell  your thumb though not perfect we like  the a pedo controllers a lot and only  wish the Super NT came bundled with one  it should be noted that even though you  won't normally have to tinker with the  8-bit doe wireless transmitter once you  started playing a game removing or  adding transmitters during gameplay can  occasionally cause a super entity crash  forcing you to power the console off and  back on  according to analog this is due to  related power demands coming from the  transmitter in the system detecting and  protecting the fpga from potential harm  thankfully wired controllers can be  connected and disconnected without  shutting down the system while there are  obviously a few quirks to keep in mind  the benefits of the super NTA are  outweigh it's relatively minor issues  there is simply no other console that  can play actual Super Nintendo  cartridges at 1080p without relying on  expensive video equipment and looking  beyond resolution the number of settings  at your fingertips makes it easy to dial  in the look you want for your SNES games  in a matter of seconds the Super NT is  impressive both inside and out and a  smart solution for anyone who strives to  see their favorite 16-bit Nintendo games  in their best light  [Music]
 

